186045417] [17592186124464 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124464 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124464 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124464 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124464 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1045 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124731 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "00d7af21-acdd-52ae-8ffc-37290b0f0c74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125190 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125298 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125298 #edb/ref 1061 11200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35b375f8-a01b-56db-97b7-ac88f2a7883a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125306 #edb/ref 1061 11376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74d5fcb5-d32b-5ddd-92cd-491ff00e4b54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125307 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125307 #edb/ref 1061 11379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "147b9868-60b3-5d25-8663-966137fb6540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125308 #edb/ref 1061 11383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81eef922-1c17-502c-89a2-3e132f4a64e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125309 #edb/ref 1061 11384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20b0d4d3-7059-5c01-9a72-d14629d41a1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125310 #edb/ref 1061 11387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "266600d8-59f0-586e-ba73-083efb5b7259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125735 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125735 #edb/ref 1065 2499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125735 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125735 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125740 #edb/ref 1064 "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125740 #edb/ref 1065 2524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125740 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125740 #edb/ref 1067 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263005 #edb/ref 1080 "2x-y-5=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263005 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263005 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263009 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263009 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263009 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263024 #edb/ref 1080 "x-1=y-2=z-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263068 #edb/ref 1080 "x-3y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263068 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592